  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 2ee87e681215b6147a44021cf30db039, type: 3}
  m_Name: 4.Wells Street
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  nameOfPoint: Wells Street
  coordinatesPoint: {x: 14.517546, y: 35.89833}
  firstPartInfo: "These days, Wells Street is one of the few corners of Valletta
    that still belongs primarily to the city\u2019s residents, and that hasn\u2019t
    yet been touched by the wave of gentrification that has transformed the city\u2019s
    main thoroughfares.   Standing on this quiet street, it\u2019s difficult to imagine
    the horror that gripped this side of the city in 1813. Of course, the plague
    reached every corner of Valletta, and even the towns and villages beyond its
    walls were not safe from its reach. Yet nowhere did the epidemic take such a
    toll as it did on this narrow street. The Government Gazette reports that, on
    the 31st of May, five people fell ill on this street alone - the next day, two
    of them were already dead. No wonder the newspaper referred to it as a \u201Cwretched\u201D
    street at the time.   <link=Additional_2><u><color=blue>Additional Information.</link></u></color>"
  secondPartInfo: 'Yet a question remains. How did the plague get to Malta in the
    first place, in 1813? The outbreak began a year earlier, far from these shores
    - in Constantinople, to be precise. From there it spread to other ports in the
    Ottoman Empire, including Alexandria in Egypt - but how did it make the leap
    from there, to this corner of Valletta?

    The archive yields the solution
    once again. In the Government Gazette of the time, one can read the names and
    addresses of the plague victims. If I trace them in reverse chronological order,
    I can find the starting point.'
  imgMarker: {fileID: 21300000, guid: c98feaebd40d8df449e6d3855ac84352, type: 3}
  imgMain: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f26fa8880b3aeef4b8516c82dc12f2a5, type: 3}
  nameOfStory: Quarantine
  hintOfPOI: "(cat)  Oh the street you\u2019re looking for is named after a saint
    the people love here in Valletta. His feast day is the 10th of February, and
    I end up spending the day hiding under parked cars - those loud fireworks and
    marching bands scare my nine lives out of me! "
  solutionOfPOI: "St. Paul\u2019s street is what you\u2019re looking for. It\u2019s
    named in honour of the church of St. Paul\u2019s Shipwreck - make your way there. "
